Hubbard Compressor Station
Restructuring Proposal
1/25/00

Purpose: 

Restructure the Hubbard Compressor Station Compression Services Agreement to increase the heat rate in exchange for a new heat rate schedule that effectively removes minimum required gas deliveries from the agreement.

Note: all gas prices and spreads are notional until booked.

Proposal:

This proposal has two parts.  Part A increases the heat rate in exchange for a cash payment and a release from minimum volume deliveries without changing the expected load profile of the equipment.  Part B carries Part A one step further, in that it establishes a billing load factor of 100%, and creates a yearly rebate for energy not consumed.

Part A

Modify the Compression Services Agreement so that article 3.3, “Monthly Fuel Gas Payable”, and article 1.1, “Definitions” results in the following:

“Actual Monthly Fuel Gas Payable” becomes 0.01525 MMBtu/hp-hr when the station load factor is greater than the expected load factor.  The Expected Load Factor is listed in Exhibit 1.
Include a conversion table that lists the Fuel Gas Payable when the monthly load factor is less than the Expected Load factor.  The Fuel Gas Payable rate will reach a maximum at 15% Load Factor, and will decrease to zero when the Load Factor is zero.

This modification has the effect of increasing the expected yearly gas deliveries to ECS by approximately 208,320 MMBtu/y.

In addition to removing the minimum required monthly deliveries, ECS will pay Northern $3,875,000 for increasing the Monthly Fuel Gas Payable rate.  This value is net of approximately $0.034/MMBtu to cover transaction expenses.

Part B

Modify the Compression Services Agreement so that article 3.3 results in an arrangement whereby Northern pays the Fuel Gas Payable rate listed in Part A according to a Load Factor of 100% each month.

In addition to the consideration paid in Part A, ECS will pay Northern a cash rebate for energy not consumed at the end of each year.  If Northern uses horsepower hours in accordance with the expected load factors in Exhibit 1, the expected rebate at the end of 2000 would be $579,800.  For the full year of 2001, the expected rebate would be $1,023,000.  These rebates correspond to a cash value of $0.0365/hp-hr that is not used during the period.  The period is defined according to the gas delivery schedule, which is delayed from the energy used schedule by two months.  These rebates would be paid through the full term of the existing Compression Services Agreement.

In further consideration of levelizing the gas stream and increasing the overall deliveries of gas at Ventura, the payment described in Part A will be increased by $413,000.  This additional value accrues due to Enron’s current trading position at that delivery point.  The total consideration paid under Part B, not including yearly rebate checks, will be $4,288,000.

Unwinding Expenses

Modify the Compression Services Agreement so that if the compressor station is operated at a monthly load factor of less than 15% for three consecutive months, that the Compression Services Agreement will be terminated.  At termination, Northern will be responsible for all termination costs associated with financing of the original payment to Northern, and any and all unwinding costs associated with price hedges put into place to protect the gas price valuations reflected in the cash payments described in this restructuring proposal.  Since unwinding costs are a function of the market price of gas at the time that the hedges are unwound, it is not possible to accurately determine the potential expense at this time.

Terms

The prices and values reflected in this proposal assume that the Compression Services Agreement will be modified and in effect for HP-Hrs consumed during the month of June 2000.  The modification will remain in effect until the termination of the original Compression Services Agreement.

Applicability

This proposal is applicable for one month after the proposal date.







